# Project Instructions (Claude Code)

This project follows the `engineering-workflow` methodology. Before doing
any work, read, in this order:

1. `.engineering-workflow/workflow.config.json` — the project contract:
   verify/test/build commands, default risk level, spec tool in use.
2. `openspec/` (if `spec_tool.type` is `openspec` in the contract) — the
   current specification of work.
3. `git log` and `git status` — what has already changed.
4. The project's own test/verify output — never assume tests pass without
   running the command declared in the contract.
5. `.engineering-workflow/HANDOFF.md`, if present — a **disposable**
   operational note from a previous session. It accelerates continuity but
   is never a durable source of truth; if it conflicts with git/tests/docs,
   those win.

Lifecycle: DISCOVER → SHAPE → SPEC → IMPLEMENT → VERIFY → REVIEW → DOCUMENT
→ HANDOFF/COMMIT. Full definition:
`<path to engineering-workflow clone>/methodology/OVERVIEW.md`.

Risk gates (see `methodology/RISK-MODEL.md`): LOW → verify. MEDIUM → verify
+ review. HIGH → verify + independent review. CRITICAL → verify +
independent review + durable human approval.

If the `codex` plugin (codex-plugin-cc) is installed, `/codex:review` and
`/codex:adversarial-review` MAY be used as the independent-review step for
HIGH/CRITICAL work. Its absence must never block work — fall back to a
human reviewer or another agent.

Before ending a session with incomplete work, update
`.engineering-workflow/HANDOFF.md` with current state and next steps.
